  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 315 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe long term discussion remains mostly the same with little to talk \nabout on the front end other than some lovely Fall weather until we \nget to Halloween, where rain chances will start to increase a bit. \nThere does seem to be some increasing probabilities that everyone \nwill see some rain that is more than just a few sprinkles with the \nweekend system.\n\nGoing into Wednesday night, surface high pressure over the Northeast \nwill move off the east coast into the Atlantic. Temps will warm a \nbit, into the mid to upper 70s for highs on Thursday, but high \npressure will continue to keep us dry. A very quick moving clipper-\nlike system will move across the Great Lakes and Northeast on \nThursday, squeezing our current airmass between dry, cold air to the \nnorth and the more subtropical air well to our south. This air mass \nwill modify, warming a bit more through mixing process on Friday and \nbringing temperatures at or above 10 degrees above normal in some \nlocations across north and central Georgia. Increasing cloud cover \ninto Friday night will mean temperatures only get down into the mid \nto upper 50s, compared to the widespread 40s we've been experiencing.\n\nSome uncertainty still remains around timing for rainfall moving \ninto Saturday and Sunday. Strong upper level system will move out of \nthe Northeast, driving another surface high along the Appalachians \nthat should bring another wedge/CAD event into the CWA. Machine \nLearning guidance definitely leans into a pretty decent wedge, \nperhaps showing some needed bias correction for the current models. \nEither way, rain chances start to show up between 18Z and 00Z, \npicking up even more overnight into Sunday. The GFS ensemble seems a \nbit of an outlier in terms of a faster timing for the rain, but we \nshall see. Still worth preparing for some potential showers during \ntrick-or-treat, especially in parts of western Georgia. Once rain \ndoes move through into Sunday, probabilities of greater than 0.1\" \nare starting to move up, showing widespread 70+% across Georgia. \nCurrent forecast would bring a quarter to half an inch of rain to \nsome areas by the time the system moves out Sunday evening.",
